Well, I was raised on speech-act theory and so I would maintain that "I hop=
e he=20
comes" has two different uses, can be used in two different speech acts,=20
corresponding pretty much to the two in Spanish and Lojban.  It is this=20
possibility that leads to so many problems with UI and some other classes. =
 The=20
difference of course is just whether the sentence can be true or false or n=
ot:=20
the expressive ('a'o klama') cannot, since it makes no claim and is not eve=
n a=20
reliable guide to whether I hope this or not.  The other ('mi pacna lo nu=
=20
klama') is false if I don't really have that hope (both are, of course, tot=
ally=20
independent of whether he comes or not).  People jump from the fact that th=
e=20
'a'o' form is in English "I hope' to using 'a'o' also for the 'pacna' form =
and=20
then generalizing to all UI and beyond.  I agree that "emotion" is a bad wo=
rd=20
for what UI does, but there isn't any good word, since it covers a wide ran=
ge of=20
speech acts: performative, expressive of real emotions, directive, and so o=
n=20
through most of the list, possibly excluding declarative.  Maybe "expressio=
n" is=20
a bad word, too, since it is after all a common word for any linguistic=20
productionm but again. there are not a lot of good choices here if we want =
to=20
cover the range (doing which may be the original bad idea - trying to cover=
 a=20
syntactic class with a pragmatic classification).




